[interviewer · WRAP · 10:15] Okay. Beyond those levers, what else could the client do, whether near-term or longer-term? Then pull it together into what you would tell the CEO. That isn't something I have data on.

[candidate · 11:45] Other levers: lock in longer-term bean contracts or hedge, consolidate suppliers for volume discounts, reformulate the costliest drinks, cut waste and spoilage in stores, and push loyalty or larger sizes to lift ticket size rather than headline price. Longer-term, own-brand packaging or direct sourcing from growers.

For the CEO: the margin decline is almost entirely input cost inflation that was never passed through. COGS rose from 42 to 58 points of revenue while labor and overhead were flat, and menu prices haven't moved in two years even though beans are up 40% and other inputs 37.5%.

I'd recommend a staged price increase — maybe 8-10% to start, tested in a subset of the 200 stores — paired with supplier renegotiation and waste reduction. That path gets margin into the low teens without a 38% shock.

Risks: volume could drop if customers are price-sensitive, and competitors may not follow. Next step would be an elasticity test in a few markets and a full bean sourcing review.
