[interviewer · WRAP · 11:48] What's the biggest risk to that recommendation, and how would you test for it?

[candidate · 13:18] The biggest risk is demand elasticity — if a 10% price rise drives more than about 10% of volume away, they're worse off than before, and specialty coffee has cheap substitutes right on the same street.

I'd test it by piloting the increase in maybe 20 of the 200 stores for a couple of months, picking a mix of urban and suburban markets, and comparing transaction volume and revenue per store against matched control stores that keep old prices. That tells you the actual drop-off rather than guessing.

I'd also watch whether customers trade down to cheaper drinks rather than leaving entirely, since mix shift could eat the gain quietly.

If volume holds, roll it out; if it falls hard, lean more on the supplier and waste levers instead.
